30037 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430037 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430037 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430037 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430037 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430037 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186430025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430037 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cd353f17-4f1a-5904-b62b-8c4277a7e856" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430041 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a7/a70a12201e99ded8eed85b797c760862814e1f0a50129020fa6d3b35d4334394.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts (see figure above). What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5eff5095-a0c0-5448-9e3d-e45ec11b1792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430041 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =\\pm n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430044 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e501ffe3-f914-5273-b8d8-73d2e5bc3adb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430044 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})=\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}(3-x)}{3}(\\frac{x+1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-x)(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3+2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {[3x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
